# THE RUN PROGRAM. One binary, one instruction count, one peak.
#
# Welfare's runtime side used to be twenty-five counters across thirteen
# programs, each normalised against its own baseline and averaged. That shape
# priced a shape guard and a real workload the same way per counter, and it made
# the objective's runtime half an argument about which shelf a benchmark sat on.
# Ruled 2026-09-06: one consolidated program, its retired instructions are run
# speed and its peak bytes are run memory. The per-phase benchmarks all survive
# as diagnostic goldens -- they say WHERE a move came from, and this says
# whether the project came out ahead.
#
# THE MIX. Decode and encode are the bulk, because that is the work the language
# exists for and the claim the front page makes. The stress shapes are here at
# the proportion a program actually meets them: incidental beside the parse, and
# large enough that a pathology in one of them shows.
#
#   decode  98 rounds of lib/json over large.json  1,045,772,072  34.54%
#   encode  90 rounds over the same document        1,042,585,953  34.43%
#   deep    545 trials of the nested grid             188,771,447   6.23%
#   digest  sha256 over a 15,675-byte slice           152,850,246   5.05%
#   index   690,000 characters walked by index        150,623,773   4.97%
#   escape  3,872 lines through the escaper           149,982,432   4.95%
#   pend    50 records with a field left pending      149,607,747   4.94%
#   split   428 characters through the splitter       147,499,570   4.87%
#                                                   -------------  ------
#   the whole program                              3,043,742,734
#
# Decode and encode carry 68.97% between them. The six shapes carry 4.87% to
# 6.23% each, near enough one another that no single pathology can hide behind
# another's noise, and small enough that none of them outweighs the parse.
#
# EVERY FIGURE ABOVE WAS MEASURED, not chosen. Each phase was built alone with
# the other seven set to zero, and its column is that build's total minus the
# empty program's 11,941,844. The eight columns sum to 3,027,693,240 against a
# whole-program 3,043,742,734: the 16,049,494 difference is the file read and
# the one decode that feeds encode, which belong to no phase. Re-measure and
# rewrite this table whenever a repetition count changes.
#
# THE TABLE IS THE CONTAINER'S SITTING, and bench/instructions_golden.txt is
# CI's: 3,043,743,748 against the 3,043,742,734 above, a difference of 1,014
# instructions where glibc differs. That is 0.00003% and moves no percentage in
# the second column, so the shares stand as measured. The golden's row is the
# one a gate compares; this table is what the shares were derived from.
#
# THE COUNTS ARE NOT ROUND, and that is the point. `split` is quadratic in its
# character count -- 120 characters cost 11.8M and 1,526 cost 1.87 BILLION --
# so a round number chosen for looks put one shape at 39% of the program on the
# first sitting. Each count here is the one that landed its phase on its share.
#
# THE LIBRARY IS THE ONE THAT SHIPS. jsonbench, encodebench and widebench each
# carry a frozen copy of lib/json, deliberately, so a move in their rows is the
# compiler's rather than the library's -- and those three keep doing that job as
# diagnostics. This program imports std/json, because what the project costs to
# run includes the library it ships, and a decode the objective cannot see is a
# decode nobody is paid to improve.
# THE PHASES THE PORTS ADDED. Four shapes joined on 2026-10-10, each one a
# cliff that a port of a real program fell off natively while the interpreter
# ran it in a fraction of a second, and each one fixed by a pull request that
# lands after this table does. The corpus-first gavel of 2026-09-05 asks for
# exactly that order: the shape goes into the corpus first, measured on the
# compiler that still has the cliff, so the fix scores as the win it is
# instead of moving the floor.
#
# So these columns are main's, before any of the four fixes, measured the way
# the table above was: each phase built alone and the empty program's
# 6,662,610 taken off. Shares are of the whole program on the same sitting,
# 1,245,042,917.
#
#   scatter  2,340 keys counted in scrambled order   50,901,814  4.09%
#   bump     350 updates through a named read        53,247,360  4.28%
#   machine  20,000 passes of a loop in a cycle       8,825,906  0.71%
#   fold     800 pieces folded onto one string          858,398  0.07%
#
# `scatter` (kanso#1820) and `bump` (kanso#1822) are quadratic on main, and
# their counts land each near the five per cent the stress shapes above were
# given. `machine` (kanso#1824) and `fold` (kanso#1825) cost little to run
# and a great deal to hold, so their counts are sized by memory instead: each
# adds about one decoded document to the program's peak.
#
# They are not in bench/runbench_phases.txt. That file links each phase to a
# per-benchmark counter from before 2026-09-06 so history can be rebuilt, and
# nothing in that history ever measured these shapes.
#
# A fifth joined on 2026-10-11, after its fix had already merged, so its column
# is the compiler from just before that fix (main at kanso#1839) rather than
# main. Built alone the same way, less that compiler's empty program of
# 6,760,257, and shared of the whole program on it, 1,166,079,984.
#
#   restate  128 passes rebuilding a record's string   13,151,636  1.13%
#
# `restate` (kanso#1840) rebuilds a two-field record each pass with its string
# field interpolated from the last one. Before the fix, the record was
# rewritten in place below the loop's mark, where no rewind reaches what it
# points to, so every pass's string stayed. 128 passes over a 64 KiB string add
# 2 MiB to the peak, about one decoded document, like `machine` and `fold`.
# With the fix the peak does not move at all.
#
# Three more joined on 2026-10-13, from the ports #1892 tracks, each measured
# on main before its fix (main at kanso#1909) the same way: built alone, less
# the empty program's 6,766,088, and shared of the whole program on that
# compiler, 1,234,826,007.
#
#   rewind   20,000 laps of a loop entered from exec    4,020,895  0.33%
#   stack    115 chunks concatenated onto one list      2,621,918  0.21%
#   bag      4,300 pushes onto a list held in a record 58,073,973  4.70%
#
# `rewind` is bc's `for` statement (kanso#1920): a tail cycle entered by a
# tail call from the function it calls each lap, which main refuses a beat,
# so every lap's records stay. `stack` is the kv port's accumulator
# (kanso#1924), a fold that grows its list with `concat` and keeps each old
# copy until the fold returns. Each adds 2 MiB to the peak when built alone,
# and each is sized by that, like `machine` and `fold`. `bag` is the record
# accumulator four ports wrote (#1892's eleventh item): every push copies the
# list the record still holds, so it is quadratic in its count, and the count
# lands it near five per cent like `scatter` and `bump`. It adds nothing to
# the peak, because each copy is dropped on the next push.
#
# A ninth joined on 2026-10-18, before its fix (#1943), measured on main at
# kanso#1994 the same way: built alone, less the empty program's 7,482,604,
# and shared of the whole program on that compiler, 1,227,450,174.
#
#   insist   5,000 laps reading three slice! ranges   11,955,088  0.97%
#
# `insist` reads a year, a month and a word out of one line, each with
# `slice!` handed straight to `to_int` or `utf8`, the spelling a program uses
# for a range it has already bounded. Before the fix every read built the
# slice and the box between the two steps. It adds 25 bytes to the peak, the
# line it reads.
#
# Three more joined on 2026-10-25, from #2089's first, second and fourth
# items, measured on main at kanso#2092 before their fixes and shared of the
# whole program on that compiler, 917,589,885.
#
#   handed     380 updates through a read handed to a call  41,508,775  4.52%
#   guarded    3,050 pushes onto a record's list, guarded   43,734,573  4.77%
#   overwrite  380 laps reading and overwriting 16 keys     42,238,603  4.60%
#
# `handed`'s and `overwrite`'s columns are built alone, less the empty
# program's 329,467 and 327,245.
# `guarded`'s is not, and cannot be: its cost is a cliff whose knee depends
# on what the arena already holds. Built alone, 3,050 pushes sit below the
# knee and cost 746,860; the knee there is at 4,096 elements. Inside this
# program the knee falls between 2,000 and 3,000, so its column is the whole
# program with it less the whole program without it.
#
# `handed` is `bump` with the name passed to a function inside the put,
# `seen = m[i]` then `put m i (counted seen)`, which toml, datalog, diff, bc
# and inflate all wrote. kanso#1822 discounts the name only when it reaches
# the write through arithmetic, so every put here copies the map, and the
# phase is superlinear in its count like `bump` was.
# `guarded` is `bag` written the way markdown's port wrote it, under a
# `return st if i > n` guard. kanso#1926 moves the list out of the record
# only when every mention of the record is a field read, and the guard's
# early answer is a mention that is not, so every lap copies the list.
# `overwrite` is chip8's memory: a map of 16 keys in a record, read and then
# overwritten once a lap. Every put copies the map's whole append log, and
# the log keeps each overwrite, so it grows by an entry a lap and the phase
# is quadratic in its laps.
#
# One more joined on 2026-10-25, from #2089's third item, measured on main at
# kanso#2092 before its fix. Its column is the whole program with it,
# 988,276,908, less the whole without, 917,694,203, and its share is of the
# former.
#
#   reentered  62 writes through a loop re-entered       70,582,705  7.14%
#
# `reentered` is the lox port's statement loop when a Lox constructor builds
# a tree: a loop whose lap enters the same loop again, carrying a persistent
# trie that each lap writes one leaf into. Every fresh entry staged its carry
# on its first lap, because the lap rule measured the arena's drift from
# where the last loop at its depth had left it. Its cost is a knee like
# `guarded`'s. Built alone it costs 1,236,018 with the empty program's
# start-up, and inside this program each of those stages also walks what the
# earlier phases left behind. Depth 3 sits below the knee and adds 1,254,367,
# so 4 is the smallest depth that reaches it.
#
# Two more joined on 2026-10-25, found while profiling the run program after
# #2106, measured on main at that merge before their fixes. Each column is
# the whole program with both, 880,945,062, less the whole without that one,
# and the shares are of the former.
#
#   branched  22,305 pushes through an `if` arm          40,556,036  4.60%
#   padded    75 rows padded to a widening width         42,367,338  4.81%
#
# `branched` is `guarded` with the loop's end written as an `if` expression,
# whose arm that answers the record whole kept the list from being moved out
# of it, so every push copied the list once it passed the arena's knee. The
# knee here falls between 22,290 and 22,310 pushes, below it the phase is
# linear at about 260 instructions a push, and past it each push costs about
# a million and a half, so the count sits a few pushes past it.
# `padded` lays out a column with `text/pad_start`, whose padding grew one
# copy of the fill at a time and counted its characters on every step: a row
# cost the square of its width. Its count lands it near five per cent like
# the stress shapes above.
#
# One more joined on 2026-10-25, found by a probe that timed each list, text
# and map function at two sizes, measured on main at kanso#2112 before its
# fix. Its column is the whole program with it, 843,728,377, less the whole
# without, 799,281,633, and its share is of the former.
#
#   counted   2,000 words counted, each twice            44,446,744  5.27%
#
# `counted` is a word count with `list/tally` over words that each come
# round twice in no order. A put of a key the map already held walked the
# map's append log back from its end to find that key's entry, so the second
# sighting of each word cost the number of words counted so far.
#
# Two more joined on 2026-10-25 from the same probe, measured on kanso#2116's
# tree before their fixes. Each column is the whole program with both,
# 894,638,261, less the whole without that one, and the shares are of the
# former.
#
#   drained   550 deletes from a map of 1,100 keys       46,548,701  5.20%
#   admitted  430 keys admitted to a set once each       46,965,802  5.25%
#
# `drained` fills a map and takes half its keys out again with `delete`, in
# no order. `delete` answers a fresh map, so each call copied the whole map
# on both engines and draining a map cost the square of its size.
# `admitted` keeps a set as a map and puts a key only when the map does not
# hold it, testing `m[k] == none` in an `if`. The read in the condition
# counted as a second holder, so the put copied the map, and the copy had no
# sorted view for the next read, which sorted the whole map again.
#
# One more joined on 2026-10-25 from the same probe, measured on kanso#2119's
# tree before its fix. Its column is the whole program with it, 855,640,794,
# less the whole without, 809,929,455, and its share is of the former.
#
#   deduped   850 words kept once each, in first order   45,711,339  5.34%
#
# `deduped` is `admitted` with the set kept in a record beside the list of
# words it admits. The read of the set in the condition, and the arm that
# hands the record back unchanged, each counted as another holder of the
# record, so the set and the list were both copied for every word.
#
# One more joined on 2026-10-25 from the same probe, measured on main at
# kanso#2121 before its fix. Its column is the whole program with it,
# 851,889,184, less the whole without, 810,763,317, and its share is of the
# former.
#
#   nested    1,700 counts kept in a map of maps          41,125,867  4.83%
#
# `nested` counts pairs of keys in a map of 50 inner maps. Natively both
# puts copy, so every count copied the outer map and its inner map. Here the
# inner maps stay small and the phase grows about linearly; at 20,000 counts
# the probe took 2.28 billion instructions.
import "./admitted"
import "./bag"
import "./branched"
import "./bump"
import "./counted"
import "./deduped"
import "./deep"
import "./drained"
import "./escape"
import "./fold"
import "./guarded"
import "./handed"
import "./index"
import "./insist"
import "./machine"
import "./nested"
import "./overwrite"
import "./padded"
import "./pend"
import "./reentered"
import "./restate"
import "./rewind"
import "./scatter"
import "./split"
import "./stack"
import "std/io"
import "std/json"
import "std/sha256"
import "std/text"

decode_rounds = 98

encode_rounds = 90

deep_trials = 545

pend_cards = 50

escape_lines = 3872

index_chars = 690000

split_chars = 428

digest_bytes = 15675

scatter_keys = 2340

bump_steps = 350

machine_passes = 20000

fold_pieces = 800

restate_passes = 128

rewind_laps = 20000

stack_laps = 115

bag_items = 4300

handed_steps = 380

guarded_pushes = 3050

overwrite_laps = 380

reentered_depth = 4

insist_laps = 5000

branched_pushes = 22305

padded_rows = 75

counted_words = 2000

drained_keys = 1100

admitted_keys = 430

deduped_words = 850

nested_counts = 1700

fn decoded _ 0 sofar
  sofar

fn decoded raw n sofar
  decoded raw (n - 1) (sofar + length (json/decode raw))

fn encoded _ 0 sofar
  sofar

fn encoded doc n sofar
  encoded doc (n - 1) (sofar + length (json/encode doc))

fn digested raw n
  hashed (text/slice raw 1 n)

# `n` is at most the length of `raw`, so the slice is never none
fn hashed none
  0

fn hashed piece
  length (sha256/hex (text/bytes piece))

# `decoded` can answer the decode's err, and `+` wants a value, so the sum
# dispatches on it first.
fn summed e@(err _) _
  e

fn summed a b
  a + b

fn tally raw doc a b
  bulk = summed (decoded raw decode_rounds 0) (encoded doc encode_rounds 0)
  shapes = a + b + escape/total escape_lines + index/total index_chars
  scanned = split/total split_chars
  ported = scatter/total scatter_keys + bump/total bump_steps
  ported2 = machine/total machine_passes + fold/total fold_pieces
  ported3 = ported2 + restate/total restate_passes
  ported4 = rewind/total rewind_laps + stack/total stack_laps
  ported5 = ported3 + ported4 + bag/total bag_items + handed/total handed_steps
  ported6 = ported5 + guarded/total guarded_pushes
  ported7 = ported6 + overwrite/total overwrite_laps
  ported8 = ported7 + reentered/total reentered_depth
  ported9 = ported8 + branched/total branched_pushes
  ported10 = ported9 + padded/total padded_rows
  ported11 = ported10 + counted/total counted_words
  ported12 = ported11 + drained/total drained_keys
  ported13 = ported12 + admitted/total admitted_keys
  ported14 = ported13 + deduped/total deduped_words
  ported15 = ported14 + nested/total nested_counts
  rest = digested raw digest_bytes
  on = (k -> scanned .> (s -> k + shapes + s + ported + ported15 + rest))
  bulk .> on

fn shaped raw doc a
  pend/total pend_cards .> (b -> insisted raw doc a b)

fn insisted raw doc a b
  told = (c -> io/write "runbench {tally raw doc a (b + c)}\n")
  insist/total insist_laps .> told

pub fn run raw
  on = (doc -> deep/total deep_trials .> (a -> shaped raw doc a))
  json/decode raw .> on
